<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Mirrors NexLink's palette (app/src/main/res/values/colors.xml) so the two apps
  look like siblings. §10.7 wants this moved to :shared and consumed by both —
  that is a separate, reviewable change, and doing it here would mean editing
  :shared while it carries unrelated uncommitted work.

  Prefixed `social_` so that if the two palettes ever DO land in one module, the
  merge is a rename rather than a silent collision.
-->
<!--
  LIGHT THEME. Five colours here were darkened on 2026-09-12 because
  tools/check-contrast.py found them below WCAG AA against social_bg — a §14.10
  requirement that had never been checked. They were mirrored from NexLink's
  iOS-like palette, which assumes a white background and a dark-mode-first user;
  the phone this is developed on runs dark, so nobody saw it.

  Measured before -> after, against #F2F2F7:

    social_muted       2.92 -> 4.83   (the colour of nearly every explanatory line)
    social_accent      3.67 -> 4.81
    social_danger      3.51 -> 4.81
    social_cannot_see  3.92 -> 4.81
    social_can_see     4.19 -> 4.81

  Darkened in HSV so hue and saturation are unchanged and the palette still
  reads as the same family. Targeted 4.8 rather than 4.5 so a later nudge does
  not silently drop below the line. The dark theme already passed and is
  untouched.

  social_cannot_see and social_can_see are deliberately at the SAME ratio: §9.6.1
  requires the two columns to carry equal visual weight, and fixing contrast
  must not quietly make one of them louder than the other.
-->
<resources>
    <color name="social_bg">#F2F2F7</color>
    <color name="social_surface">#FFFFFF</color>
    <color name="social_surface2">#F2F2F7</color>
    <color name="social_surface3">#E5E5EA</color>
    <color name="social_accent">#0166DA</color>
    <color name="social_accent2">#4ca3ff</color>
    <color name="social_text">#000000</color>
    <color name="social_text2">#3C3C43</color>
    <color name="social_muted">#6A6A6E</color>
    <color name="social_divider">#C6C6C8</color>
    <color name="social_danger">#BE3C40</color>
    <!-- §9.6.1 screen 2: the two columns carry equal visual weight, so these
         are deliberately the same saturation. Green-vs-red would editorialise. -->
    <color name="social_cannot_see">#1C7A43</color>
    <color name="social_can_see">#A45600</color>
    <!--
      MESSAGING CHROME (added 2026-09-15 with the §14 surface rebuild).

      Everything below is a *role*, not a shade: `social_accent_fill` is "the
      colour a filled control is painted", used by the send button, the unread
      badge and your own message bubble alike. Naming them by role is what stops
      the next change from picking a slightly different blue for each one, which
      is how the first version ended up looking assembled rather than designed.

      Every pair these form with text is in tools/check-contrast.py's PAIRS and
      passes WCAG AA in BOTH themes (§14.10). `social_accent` itself is NOT used
      as a fill: white on it measures 4.09:1 in the dark theme, which is why
      `social_accent_fill` exists and is a shade darker there.
    -->
    <color name="social_accent_fill">#0166DA</color>
    <color name="social_on_accent_fill">#FFFFFF</color>
    <!-- Timestamps inside your own bubble: legible, but quieter than the text. -->
    <color name="social_on_accent_fill_meta">#E8F1FD</color>
    <color name="social_bubble_theirs">#FFFFFF</color>
    <color name="social_bubble_meta">#6A6A6E</color>
    <!-- The inline warning card (no backup, no screen lock). Tinted rather than
         red-filled: these are standing conditions the user may live with for a
         while, and a full red block reads as an error that just happened. -->
    <color name="social_banner_bg">#FFF4E5</color>

    <!--
      Avatar tints. Five, picked by hashing the display name, so the same person
      keeps the same colour across the inbox and the timeline — that consistency
      is most of what makes an initial read as an identity rather than a letter.
      All five carry white at >= 4.5:1, so they are the same value in both
      themes: a dark-theme variant would have to be lighter, which is exactly
      the direction that breaks the text on them.
    -->
    <color name="social_on_avatar">#FFFFFF</color>
    <color name="social_avatar1">#2C6BB0</color>
    <color name="social_avatar2">#7A4FA3</color>
    <color name="social_avatar3">#1F7A5C</color>
    <color name="social_avatar4">#A8452F</color>
    <color name="social_avatar5">#5C6B8A</color>
</resources>
